{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "asset_type_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Strong grouping axis for spend analysis by asset type. Mostly well-grounded via short_description/ci (Dell Laptop->laptop, OptiPlex->desktop). But 'iPod touch / ISP Services' labeled 'cloud_service' is a misread of a mobile device, showing some ungrounded categorisation."
    },
    {
      "name": "vendor_or_manufacturer",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Vendor is a highly actionable axis for expense analysis. Values track source text well (Dell XPS->dell, OptiPlex->dell) and silent rows sensibly get other_vendor rather than an invented brand."
    },
    {
      "name": "department_assignment",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.3,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A 'department' column already exists, so this largely duplicates it. More seriously, the values appear invented: source text in the samples gives no departmental cues, yet confident specifics like 'sales', 'product_management', 'customer_support' are asserted, contradicting the silent source."
    },
    {
      "name": "processing_delay_indicator",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Bucketing processing latency is genuinely actionable for operational analysis. Derivable from processed_date vs opened_at; NULL/Unknown correctly assigned to rows with nan dates. Cannot fully verify buckets without opened_at in samples, but behaviour looks sound."
    },
    {
      "name": "asset_intended_use",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A usage axis adds some analytical leverage beyond raw asset type. Some mappings are warranted (server->server_infrastructure), but others overreach: 'iPod touch' -> 'sales_mobile' asserts a use the text does not support, and generic descriptions get confident use labels."
    },
    {
      "name": "record_generation_method",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.3,
      "source_groundedness": 0.3,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Provenance could be mildly useful, but the values are not grounded: all samples are labeled 'imported_from_source' with no source signal justifying that vs 'system_auto_generated', suggesting an unsupported guess. Near-generic and weakly evidenced."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.68,
  "source_groundedness": 0.55,
  "rationale": "The added set gives several genuinely useful axes for expense analysis (asset type, vendor, processing delay, intended use) that support grouping and comparison beyond the originals. Groundedness is mixed: asset_type, vendor, and delay are largely defensible readings of short_description/ci/dates with sensible unknowns, but department_assignment duplicates an existing column while asserting invented specifics, record_generation_method appears to guess provenance with no supporting signal, and a few asset categorisations (iPod->cloud_service/sales_mobile) misread the source. Overall useful but with notable ungrounded assertions."
}
